# docs/security-hardening/07_dependency_supply_chain_audit.md L1 (Runde 2): deny ALL
# install-time lifecycle scripts by default (root project's own pre/post/install/prepare
# scripts included, not just dependencies'). @lavamoat/allow-scripts then selectively
# re-runs only the scripts explicitly allowlisted in package.json > lavamoat.allowScripts.
#
# Consequence: `npm ci`/`npm install` no longer auto-run this project's own "postinstall"/
# "prepare" (husky), nor any dependency's own install scripts. Run `npm run allow-scripts`
# explicitly afterward (this repo's CI workflows do this as a separate step right after
# `npm ci`; local dev must do the same once per install). That command ALSO re-runs this
# project's own "install"/"postinstall"/"prepublish"/"prepare" scripts internally (see
# @lavamoat/allow-scripts' runAllowedPackages.js) — so husky's git-hooks setup still
# happens, without us wiring it separately. IMPORTANT: never name a root-level npm script
# "postinstall"/"install"/"prepublish"/"prepare" as (or as a chain including) "allow-scripts"
# itself — allow-scripts re-invokes those exact script names on the root package after it
# finishes the dependency allowlist, so doing so recurses into itself (verified 2026-09-07:
# `"postinstall": "allow-scripts && husky"` hung/failed and corrupted node_modules; fixed by
# using the distinct script name "allow-scripts" instead, which is not one of the four
# lifecycle events allow-scripts re-runs).
ignore-scripts=true
